#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138174 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/id #uuid "51b8e09e-18d4-5879-ad6c-a7ee83157115" :question/math-academy-id "e-1705" :question/problem "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\textrm{mm/min}$, what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\textrm{mm/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2327/Source/Images/ccdbdd5a53d402388d5d40e5a62095ff.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{\\textrm{change in speed}}{\\textrm{change in time}}\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{units of speed}}{\\textrm{units of time}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm/min}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138175 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/id #uuid "d1875220-7af4-5d5c-a0dc-93b1843aa4e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1706" :question/problem "A fly accelerates with constant acceleration from $20\\, \\textrm{m/min}$ to $100\\, \\textrm{m/min}$ in $2$ minutes. Find its acceleration." :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The fly starts at a speed of $20 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$. This corresponds to the point $(0,20)$.\n- In $2$ minutes, the fly has reached a speed of $100 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$. This corresponds to the point $(2,100)$.\n\nWe plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2327/Source/Images/2b37811eb25926ac410e06221003ee71.png)\n\nThe slope of the fly's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{100m/min - 20m/min}{2min - 0min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{80m/min}{2min} \\\\\n&= 40\\frac{m}{min^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the fly is $40 \\, \\textrm{m/min}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138176 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "314ed8cf-b4cf-5b4a-b168-8dcbaf1a928f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1707" :question/problem "The speed-time graph shown below represents the motion of a train. At what times is the train decelerating?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2327/Source/Images/489b979c5c4f73353276c2242f373149.png)" :question/worked-solution "The train is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from\n\n$$\nt = 15\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\nt = 40\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the train is decelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138177 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/id #uuid "ffd579d3-18eb-5204-9cf1-8c8188bfa1e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1708" :question/problem "A car accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then the car decelerates with a constant deceleration of $16 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$ until it stops. What graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0)$, since the car is starting from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$, we draw a line with slope $8$.\n\nHowever, because the car is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10$. At this time, the car's speed has increased by\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot 10 = 80 \\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nSo, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,80)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2327/Source/Images/c5449854755a0f40854024993921d5ea.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the car has a deceleration of $16 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$, so the slope of the second line segment is $-16$. The second line segment stops when the car's speed returns to $0$. In order to figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10, 80)$ and has a slope of $-16$, we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv - v_{1} &= m(t - t_{1}) \\\\\nv - 80 &= -16(t - 10) \\\\\nv - 80 &= -16t + 160 \\\\\nv &= -16t + 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the car is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= -16t + 240 \\\\\n0 &= -16t + 240 \\\\\n16t &= 240 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{240}{16} \\\\\nt &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(15,0)$. The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2327/Source/Images/c2dbea2204467d340f49c5b81ada8604.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa2eeb6-b039-5229-854e-7efb3f96e96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263511} {:answer/id #uuid "941d6c78-75be-57f3-ae76-1b5798921f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{in}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263512} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef7f8c4-92fb-5630-950d-dfef2ba12987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{in}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263513} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c218ca-1b90-5394-8f13-8cdff7341bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263514} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ff607-6037-5df6-b3ec-63f2fcb1fa81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{in}}{\\text{min}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c218ca-1b90-5394-8f13-8cdff7341bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263514} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db0d838-d4f7-5a89-a440-cc45c8e6877f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c683cff2-c81d-5571-a765-0303fbbc597c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108448" :question/problem "If the speed of a rabbit is measured in $\\text{in/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{in/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/ecdd2f132499a6be0c1d8fa48f021713e2019ed9e6660af041853f7e30766091.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{in/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7154a709-6311-5ef4-9849-968839f69e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263518} {:answer/id #uuid "d93811c7-3af9-557a-a178-31f1f9600cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263519} {:answer/id #uuid "baa91490-b964-5b05-a2fc-f5e5da0419fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263520} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1f2d97-c852-55b2-a5b6-a23f4e80e643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{min}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263521} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbee0c1-4105-5ff1-91e8-e65eadd013ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa91490-b964-5b05-a2fc-f5e5da0419fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263520} :answer-field/id #uuid "be87b783-087d-5a74-8312-c80793877db0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263517}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ce685e6-726b-5b2a-bfe1-0adc5f9a875d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108451" :question/problem "If the speed of a turtle is measured in $\\text{cm/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e525458fbae05a5ec2f7cb79240d464c7d216a977d489eff473f17c8edaa300b.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a2ad6d7-09d5-51a6-a759-0d1488257b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{mi}}^{2}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263525} {:answer/id #uuid "20e3e128-40dc-5793-9dbe-e5626fa17f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263526} {:answer/id #uuid "6360718d-2aac-5792-b587-21b9f9327881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{mi}}^{2}}{\\text{hour}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263527} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dfdc70-d2a2-5432-b656-877529c6a529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{hour}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263528} {:answer/id #uuid "72240d1d-d3f6-540d-af3b-56a891461b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72240d1d-d3f6-540d-af3b-56a891461b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263529} :answer-field/id #uuid "80723098-8ee5-5192-bd4d-9420d09886cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f246f63-5868-57f8-96c0-7964ebf48d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-108454" :question/problem "If the speed of an airplane is measured in $\\text{mi/hour},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{mi/hour}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{hours}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/10e3d32b8d1fc512f6ced10dacc5b1c3df2dc1e7564c739a459b8661c63c68c6.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{mi/hour}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a53acd1-649a-50f4-bfdd-14099d2b07e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263532} {:answer/id #uuid "ac44dea0-1f9c-514f-9582-1f8b42f6cf42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263533} {:answer/id #uuid "be7b4057-089b-58c6-9389-2a84083dfec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263534} {:answer/id #uuid "749136e7-3940-5bd4-ab48-41db4aa7b680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263535} {:answer/id #uuid "f09f261a-8722-5e14-96cd-f43d4d3ba613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be7b4057-089b-58c6-9389-2a84083dfec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263534} :answer-field/id #uuid "84804793-a8b9-58b5-8ce1-d0d8401406ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4470bd1-0bb6-5c73-9442-14ba18805f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-108456" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/352f3d049bd57a805419a34a7873f37f30c95cb2c7c28f4cac984584ff646c75.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a bus. Calculate the acceleration of the bus." :question/worked-solution "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,2),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{2\\,\\text{m/s}-0\\,\\text{m/s}}{4\\,\\text{s}-0\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,\\text{m/s}}{4\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the bus is $0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d1d84b-aa4d-500b-9211-76f7a789085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263539} {:answer/id #uuid "61cb8e6b-4381-5212-94ab-2b10ea57c883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263540} {:answer/id #uuid "44fa14bc-b4e8-538e-8cb7-95a872020aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263541} {:answer/id #uuid "db02e0dc-c22d-55da-b978-803853877155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263542} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd44afb-5e52-58cd-b17f-924c0a6ca9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d1d84b-aa4d-500b-9211-76f7a789085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263539} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d00f6a-f71e-54ef-9fe2-2388eaea42aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4ac1e14-f74c-5d5c-a627-57b051b36814" :question/math-academy-id "q-108458" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/0c1aa4aba4a5d8ea2a00bbb5e94bcc61764ed97a6bf8b434138310d45f7cd35d.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle at the start of a race. Calculate the acceleration of the motorcycle." :question/worked-solution "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(8,28),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{28\\,\\text{m/s}-0\\,\\text{m/s}}{8\\,\\text{s}-0\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28\\,\\text{m/s}}{8\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =3.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle is $3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59da84d9-8a57-5eec-851f-16b3ee5a5531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $20\\,\\text{s}$ to $40\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263546} {:answer/id #uuid "e8212280-b4ae-5e0c-b430-74c9fba115f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263547} {:answer/id #uuid "8984f22a-3ba5-538c-aec4-1e10bbe63375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263548} {:answer/id #uuid "1e120df2-39b8-55e0-ab77-62bd8e8925a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $10\\,\\text{s}$ to $50\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263549} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb66157-6550-56ca-a407-180f674c7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8212280-b4ae-5e0c-b430-74c9fba115f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263547} :answer-field/id #uuid "071e1fec-5061-5b36-9808-a0006d77444d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263545}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c47c1778-9762-5608-84e1-7206031f9c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-108462" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/31e0e843eb581f01cdc7ebb5273398a3c0f32d2949b9e36cd28423295d7b76e8.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle. At what times is the motorcycle accelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The motorcycle is accelerating when its acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=30\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=60\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle is accelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59996675-dce6-570b-8fdf-05a24af305c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{min}$ to $4\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263553} {:answer/id #uuid "11a348bb-d775-557d-98a5-1edb4dd3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $3\\,\\text{min}$ to $4\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263554} {:answer/id #uuid "39de6c91-fb21-5bae-9d55-4e8fc1cb3790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{min}$ to $3\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263555} {:answer/id #uuid "3c54109a-bacc-512d-a290-227e99a18375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $3\\,\\text{min}$ to $6\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263556} {:answer/id #uuid "1f464751-5d38-5f48-9903-e2b1bda0b7a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $4\\,\\text{min}$ to $6\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39de6c91-fb21-5bae-9d55-4e8fc1cb3790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{min}$ to $3\\,\\text{min}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263555} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22b5d9d-8c03-5c12-916c-489df846e5ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263552}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "010be3b1-d067-50b7-9732-086b5707d6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108464" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/f74fdd298fb73e3bd7e3587c18c1f6ffc8fa6062d52daa07c7158b341c6e8879.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a light aircraft. At what times is the aircraft decelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The light aircraft is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=0\\,\\text{min}$ to $t=3\\,\\text{min}.$ Therefore, the light aircraft is decelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf54c34-c89c-53a1-8b3b-183b950a56c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $5\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263560} {:answer/id #uuid "e54a8f54-54c6-52ab-8285-d352fc60f1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $8\\,\\text{s}$ to $10\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263561} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aba4cc-0d51-5483-855b-17ff4186ce4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $8\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263562} {:answer/id #uuid "b73b7795-3c87-5bcb-902f-d651b77d5426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $10\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263563} {:answer/id #uuid "78aa1668-d199-5937-b6e1-a262d6c5ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $8\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78aa1668-d199-5937-b6e1-a262d6c5ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $8\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263564} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd2099af-465d-544f-960f-1a7f84ca3251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fae0faa-74b4-5ebd-a36c-c55aba178d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-108468" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/94807e919e7e2cb3f281e53de88334bd6d50dadb1de93beb2a8adc04148c5756.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an ostrich. At what times is the ostrich decelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The ostrich is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=8\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the ostrich is decelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3706b256-e76c-5c35-a28e-24d9c25191e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263567} {:answer/id #uuid "c807cfdc-3ed2-5b23-afa3-7b95c67ff848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/1280bba15fdb6ada20f6d6f2ea4cfeeb39e9366939d72a06059062cc2abedc8d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263568} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd77db9-cd95-5827-abb5-a7923137f13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/8dd2eefb2c2397f10d4c07853ef819b8682e448824bf6e26302d4a16bc02183c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} {:answer/id #uuid "18e28070-d7b3-5b29-917b-d46773ca9cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/170dea566e8c74e1c3df3c087c6565c85dd03c0703c4dbec1be594ca653c6aa1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263570} {:answer/id #uuid "a0753367-5cbc-5c45-a98d-2965def5e03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/0c89f7f469830ff7c505d22677f6ea612f1fff396c73aba325b4e87f6f61ae42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3706b256-e76c-5c35-a28e-24d9c25191e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263567} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c930349-7cee-506d-9bd7-6c4a9268c50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263566}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e2fe401-8d6a-539f-989f-5022eb0fe1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-108567" :question/problem "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $3$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for other $3$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $1.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $3$ hours, we stop the line at $t=3.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $1\\cdot 3=3\\,\\text{km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(3,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/7a9fd32e5705860064e597fcf9308fee457063e060d3adede260f109ca488201.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $3$ hours, until $t=3+3=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e4c7e47150bf1c205c243c6fa1d0f0e24b7a69f27a8461791d495eb7487cf4f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=3,$ the particle has an acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.5.$ The third line segment stops after $3$ hours. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(6,3)$ and has a slope of $0.5,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5(t-6) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5t-3 \\\\ v & =0.5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=6+3=9$ hours, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.5t \\\\ v & =0.5(9) \\\\ v & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(9,4.5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2df05a7-1378-5172-936a-0e4daa2cbd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263574} {:answer/id #uuid "7473e063-1dec-5368-96c1-cc1a3481e6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263575} {:answer/id #uuid "f838fabb-5303-55cc-8d4b-78b6576a9962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263576} {:answer/id #uuid "064eb552-5de7-52ea-9f1e-c7ae684ff7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263577} {:answer/id #uuid "0f66a2ce-166f-5c95-b30a-ae4774bf46b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f838fabb-5303-55cc-8d4b-78b6576a9962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263576} :answer-field/id #uuid "98838d46-b56b-5a11-bde7-4d3c9b5380ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "788edb6f-366d-5629-97bf-4997d96b4e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-59502" :question/problem "A cyclist accelerates with constant acceleration from $0\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $20\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $10$ seconds. Find their acceleration." :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The cyclist starts at a speed of $0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,0).$\n\n- In $10$ seconds, the cyclist reaches a speed of $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(10,20).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e60d9d6898bd8d49fb32860c439cb022c25850ff609b80ee486e71b48203b5fc.png)\n\nThe slope of the cyclist's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}-0\\text{ m/s}}{10\\text{ s}-0\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}}{10\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cyclist's acceleration is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7f1c5ef-c6e0-5b24-b367-7eb985c5d226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263581} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bb014e-a866-5a3b-8ca3-f6c9c851e3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263582} {:answer/id #uuid "5656e317-f2f5-5327-8d15-9c41f4deb49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263583} {:answer/id #uuid "ad041e87-a577-5cfb-9e54-840eae77efcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263584} {:answer/id #uuid "a702f35e-a01a-501c-8997-7659cd5aba76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad041e87-a577-5cfb-9e54-840eae77efcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263584} :answer-field/id #uuid "84057b02-37fd-5761-b8ad-cfa5406a690d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af168475-a930-579e-8b8b-a9480bab7d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-59709" :question/problem "If the speed of a surfer is measured in $\\text{m/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{m/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/4d419f405914437764e854b63682ddb6fa4c371730a2ec1cf4263b0afd2a847e.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{m/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c70a9519-a240-5075-9773-3d95df09ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $70\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263588} {:answer/id #uuid "8a52a03e-e58b-5b97-b8b6-6922fa92d2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263589} {:answer/id #uuid "906aab22-f035-538a-b5ff-faf6115b51fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263590} {:answer/id #uuid "ef489df5-9d55-5642-a7f9-138ff2bd96d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263591} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ffe1d3-964e-5537-9b57-183d923dd996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ffe1d3-964e-5537-9b57-183d923dd996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263592} :answer-field/id #uuid "377113c7-0809-5b74-92bc-970b36c9eb83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cca94ba1-21b2-5b66-81e8-07381d34c86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-59711" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/40143c20622029d70a978310a7d38bc41d7b8411151d3d5f61a3b280e5807841.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an object. At what times is the object accelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The object is accelerating when its acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the object is accelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f53e87-1984-559e-aaba-493370ccfbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $7\\,\\text{s}$ to $9\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263595} {:answer/id #uuid "3b029dee-186d-53bb-86da-8803a59fee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $3\\,\\text{s}$ to $5\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263596} {:answer/id #uuid "ff88f1e1-c157-5a8f-ab8e-3568e5c36c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $3\\,\\text{s}$ to $5\\,\\text{s}$ and from $7\\,\\text{s}$ to $9\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263597} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef84479-b3ce-542c-87f3-760c29c26a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $3\\,\\text{s}$ and from $7\\,\\text{s}$ to $9\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263598} {:answer/id #uuid "841a4aad-0667-5372-9350-70fc224ca011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $3\\,\\text{s}$ and from $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $7\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "841a4aad-0667-5372-9350-70fc224ca011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $3\\,\\text{s}$ and from $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $7\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263599} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a98cff3-74fa-520f-b311-46b0c2a5d66c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0f780b9-e217-582a-b4c6-39ce247aadff" :question/math-academy-id "q-59714" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/0bfadc49b3d5d846641b78e07a6ec279b11da13e6f69dec3f1f054e03d5d3f6d.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a windsurfer. At what times is the windsurfer accelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The windsurfer is accelerating when their acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ and from $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=7\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the motorcycle is windsurfer in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904c8abf-e5af-59d0-86f5-c5752de23468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263602} {:answer/id #uuid "f17bc2dc-c8ff-5573-bb4c-2398c1104a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263603} {:answer/id #uuid "d383b76d-7fe0-5500-baaa-ff7247df1f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263604} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3dfe79-0aa2-5776-b0de-d5cfc3b10d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263605} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5d046d-da80-5f47-a99c-07ef0cfb912a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904c8abf-e5af-59d0-86f5-c5752de23468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263602} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d6356c4-e0e8-5ece-8e38-7d0011279fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3137b36-6d19-5c82-80f3-7ce7d93599be" :question/math-academy-id "q-59725" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/700081e764cbf4be23f702b1c136ba2e4b6b1f460c96f57e2898c3ac070e62bb.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a surfer. Calculate the acceleration of the surfer." :question/worked-solution "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(8,5),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}-0\\text{ m/s}}{8\\text{ s}-0\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}}{8\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the surfer is $\\frac{5}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381bb04e-5bdb-5dfe-bc1f-de78472f064d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263609} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4dc282-4fbe-5f15-acc5-deffa034334c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263610} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad0c358-0022-522d-a373-178f2392f3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263611} {:answer/id #uuid "b977adf4-f4ba-5c82-9a98-669c1dbb2000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263612} {:answer/id #uuid "bca52c30-480b-5645-9def-8226a27baafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b977adf4-f4ba-5c82-9a98-669c1dbb2000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263612} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b726e35-2998-5856-be8f-0b76d1180dc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3681f60-e73d-553c-92d2-8946be3cb315" :question/math-academy-id "q-59749" :question/problem "A cyclist accelerates with a constant acceleration from $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $12\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $4$ seconds. Find their acceleration." :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The cyclist starts at a speed of $2\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,2).$\n\n- In $4$ seconds, the cyclist reaches a speed of $12\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(4,12).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d1a813ebcbfa04b42729d60b73bc439a2060aafae8430c60984ba1772e940364.png)\n\nThe slope of the cyclist's speed-time graph gives their acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{12\\text{ m/s}-2\\text{ m/s}}{4\\text{s}-0\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\text{ m/s}}{4\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cyclist's acceleration is $2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191eeee1-b661-51a1-b6e4-9127696b474c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263616} {:answer/id #uuid "43c1a9a4-1553-5b82-a4e1-d59493d4622f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d1123b8a9a86288380e084f8001ef5ebcc160d5647c83f3878a3d7b357087a9a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd047f9-c7f2-5820-8567-c1741e0ae2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/7c3e6680c0a158181e11b473a94fa43b1a2afb3ea5c6206244801f4830bafff8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263618} {:answer/id #uuid "19cb75f0-a33b-5df0-9c1e-6052290b754b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/11691073c02c80a9c4400623a8ee648d8a9405faab89ac8ce4fdbb7dbdd61baa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263619} {:answer/id #uuid "b650d23d-75fa-5b40-9dce-dcf65c8a210c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/ec8ff99395b5f33f531303acd91c58ada3b7f71213a5af5c76c86dae62d277e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191eeee1-b661-51a1-b6e4-9127696b474c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263616} :answer-field/id #uuid "be97c182-d747-5fd5-a932-3391e9e53795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3482860b-89cd-5fe9-aa14-3b6ba9d6957f" :question/math-academy-id "q-59760" :question/problem "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $4$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $5.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $4$ hours, we stop the line at $t=4.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $4\\cdot 5=20\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(4,20).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/cf0471f38486963f4237b4fab7c1cd109d02cc2d7ac3e30990831654f04c5b11.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. So, the speed remains at $20\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $6$ hours, until $t=4+6=10.$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(10,20).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "916e6da9-a2d1-5bf9-9777-ce4ca4858b96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Units of Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85986805-9eb9-571d-8eab-436cb4c6bc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263623} {:answer/id #uuid "5587e043-9304-5405-93be-c58b2410f20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263624} {:answer/id #uuid "f2291df0-8ab3-52ce-8fda-1513ad595073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263625} {:answer/id #uuid "f692c834-e620-5135-9193-26d7fcee5b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263626} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc97691-63d5-5aab-99d7-571e3927d025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f692c834-e620-5135-9193-26d7fcee5b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263626} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27454ab-8e79-5daa-9d39-d80fb1fda56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32993449-a772-5d35-ad65-f08d575152d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-91439" :question/problem "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\text{cm/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/f97581877814a2626d820b07b4e7a2c3d637f9369b119d0e0c3896c77e15f964.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3fa526c-68a7-5a81-a6f8-edb96f0a9212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263630} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff10561-fbf1-52a4-8777-c0e00d9bccaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/31477705bc3c0daa54aa8dda45680d1d8381d2bd720d920868780796f58f05bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263631} {:answer/id #uuid "f772a2bd-3055-569f-ba38-8193320172f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bf57601cb3fd2fc47b4b69947acc8311f25100655b9f8e40b906b8c2af8502cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263632} {:answer/id #uuid "c98edf1e-ef62-5c5c-9266-82a9e374a5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/32aa87f487c2d0082e0e3ceeebf037d8cdb3052a726fc5b43902d621dfdec790.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263633} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6a5bff-0668-54ac-ac2a-8f9a08e49f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/3d4a5d8faed32c63b39d7ca288030b27ce35bb4bfe1fbccb47072ec45ea2c2d3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3fa526c-68a7-5a81-a6f8-edb96f0a9212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263630} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4eb0a6b-0aa2-5e9b-8bc7-86fba7d5e43d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0137214-9cb6-52dd-8cad-70440ba49638" :question/math-academy-id "q-91537" :question/problem "A man accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of his motion. He then jogs at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for another $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the man starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $0.3.$ \n\nHowever, because the man is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the man's speed has increased by $0.3\\cdot 10=3\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/4a6d3b2d3f1664aed35ea11b064f6f75a3707a2051414ca9015a690148f06c16.png)\n\nThe man then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=10+20=30.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/1b579d714470aa484c23447647864b165c5b453e56e56262ace4c6d5d6b7d5f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=30$, the man has an acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.1.$ The third line segment stops after $20$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(30,3)$ and has a slope of $0.1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1(t-30) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1t-3 \\\\ v & =0.1t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=30+20=50$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.1t \\\\ v & =0.1(50) \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(50,5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "478f4adc-b642-5f2a-ae49-cbe9ab45ee81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/60491436cdf458f0f71ccaa114120150f4a7bd54552e99d95a169c5149765b98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263637} {:answer/id #uuid "abb091c4-dd31-5753-b9bd-0c469190b11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/02f41321ebec8a6a564fbd17ee9f86385817b7f767fd56fe9020a45ca2c39338.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263638} {:answer/id #uuid "a751bd80-f6b8-5982-871d-4cd2115b136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263639} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e6daa0-bcf8-5286-95ad-3eef5e99eb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e20692bb628a6ceebc9b3ce1ae89508c79b4f07da9a97e92a847ca9d9e958b45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263640} {:answer/id #uuid "105ccda9-840a-51bf-ba6f-ea4ae61501b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d9b3b459f9dbe86701640a1ee01f100efe057e0aa2d656679e6761e9338d9edb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a751bd80-f6b8-5982-871d-4cd2115b136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263639} :answer-field/id #uuid "145c2b2b-a152-5682-a275-3cc9fe597105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263636}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee3c269b-662d-5df4-aa3f-23055e6c89fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-91547" :question/problem "A school bus accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $30\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $2$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours before decelerating with a constant deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ until it stops. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the school bus starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $30\\,{\\text{km/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $30.$ \n\nHowever, because the school bus is only accelerating for the first $2$ hours, we stop the line at $t=2.$ At this time, the school bus's speed has increased by $2\\cdot 30=60\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(2,60).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2126d713b00d66abb0d06e5878fdadc543c9c8a3a0000eb2f56b1048a2824ca4.png)\n\nThe school bus then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours. So, the speed remains at $60\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $2$ hours, until $t=2+2=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/6512a9d80ca9de16f5384031590b7ae5e58a9a2871bd5ac7aace1803f0d82603.png)\n\nAfter $t=4$, the school bus has a deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $-20.$ The third line segment stops when the school bus's speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(4,60)$ and has a slope of $-20,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-60 & =-20(t-4) \\\\ v-60 & =-20t+80 \\\\ v & =-20t+140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the school bus is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-20t+140 \\\\ 0 & =-20t+140 \\\\ 20t & =140 \\\\ t & =\\frac{140}{20} \\\\ t & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(7,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e38f1794-4b1e-5c90-b4e7-cb61382178cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/36d496e2c8e91564532d493f27dbf3fd1d115efddbd61ec4ff8a0d77fa0a88e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263644} {:answer/id #uuid "6e426c38-41ac-51b8-ac5c-1836d411514e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b1ece8dd03146aaf420746b6ba3cc59b3d707150fe3682bbdcd6f05d645fb4c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263645} {:answer/id #uuid "48d714d1-ac3e-545a-946d-ace5d2e25e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/f94ce9ffcd4e0a6cd3348dd5e673effe961c3899ba4824323504ee903372727b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263646} {:answer/id #uuid "acc6a84a-fb8a-5200-a698-0557a9effe51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/6aaee2706e33b0d620e4d9fd323ba2ccac713dc06ce954eb7e8812a16ea8f99b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263647} {:answer/id #uuid "49d8ad59-6cb1-528b-be81-4fe8a7737b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b0ee977d6368c0ed1e936b01d49086e253af032b25e9fd88a80e11ebe1beee5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e426c38-41ac-51b8-ac5c-1836d411514e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b1ece8dd03146aaf420746b6ba3cc59b3d707150fe3682bbdcd6f05d645fb4c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263645} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae954b20-7e4d-5b4d-ae0c-450fa3a16ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263643}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fbae218-1c51-5854-8184-ffd9f720ee73" :question/math-academy-id "q-91549" :question/problem "A train accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then the train decelerates with a constant deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the next $15$ seconds, before traveling at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" :question/worked-solution "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the train starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $3.$ \n\nHowever, because the train is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the train's speed has increased by $10\\cdot 3=30\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,30).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/54ac34c5fe7833d42fb8ddda729d3823ff0e726e1e6228492639a9e91bad2762.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the train has a deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the second line segment is $-1.$ The second line segment stops after $15$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10,30)$ and has a slope of $-1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-30 & =-(t-10) \\\\ v-30 & =-t+10 \\\\ v & =-t+40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second line segment stops when $t=10+15=25$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-t+40 \\\\ v & =-25+40 \\\\ v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b199170cfdadd688a15668afdbac796a90fc158c94bfc0ffc8b9245c5a741e8e.png)\n\nThe train then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $15\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=25+20=45.$\n\nFinally, the third line segment stops at the point $(45,15).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b1ece8dd03146aaf420746b6ba3cc59b3d707150fe3682bbdcd6f05d645fb4c4.png)"}]}
